.TH CHTTPSVR_SET_ENGINE_MEM_MGMT_PROCS 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs \- set custom memory management for engine-level allocations
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sends every allocation of the shared ccol_event_loop reactor to the
malloc, free, calloc and realloc that mp holds.  Those allocations
come from the construction of the reactor, and from the records that
it keeps for each connection registration.  This works the same way as
the memory management procs that every other module in this library
accepts.  A call with NULL goes back to the default allocator.

Each chttpsvr instance uses its own allocator for its own connections
and requests, and you set that one with ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp, under
the usual _mp convention.  This function is separate from that
allocator.  It affects only the construction of the one shared
reactor.

You can call this function only before the first chttpsvr_start call
in the process.  After the reactor allocates memory with one set of
procs, a swap to a different malloc and free pair corrupts the heap.
You can call it again after the reactor stops completely, that is,
after chttpsvr_engine_wait returns, and before the next
chttpsvr_start.

All four function pointers (malloc, free, calloc and realloc) must be
set when mp is not NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when mp is not NULL but has a NULL function pointer.
ccol_not_permitted when the reactor already runs.  Stop it first in
                  that case.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_set_engine_logger (3),
.BR chttpsvr_set_engine_num_reactor_threads (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
